Description of activities:
Kenya requested follow-up mission for continuous training in data reporting due to challenges faced with compiling the data. The main objectives of the mission to Kenya were to: 
      - review the challenges faced by Kenya in reporting fisheries data; 
      - provide guidance on the various IOTC reporting tools; 
      - provide training on data submission through the e-MARIS system; 
      - follow up on the development of the national fisheries management system.

Following requests from the Scientific Committee, and subsequently, the Commission to review the Omani data magament system to understand the substantial increased in yellowfin tuna, the Secretarait were invitated by Oman to assess their system. The objectives of the mission were to: 
- Understand updates made by MAFWR’s data and statistics department in line with current IOTC reporting requirements, including the identification of data reporting gaps and bottlenecks; 
- Examine the progress made in data handling and data reporting processes, with specific focus on the recently developed database for industrial fisheries; 
- Examine methodologies currently used by MAFWR’s data and statistics department, and identify opportunities for further enhancing the reporting process to the IOTC; 
- Examine the current port sampling systems in place, including sampling coverage and any potential gaps in data collection; 
- Observe the implementation of field data collection processes at selected landing sites together with colleagues from MAFWR’s data and statistics department.
